PUBLIC RESPONSE: {H}

The pattern of blanks is identical across every known copy of the Bansenshukai, which already rules out explanations that require independent physical degradation (G) or independent moral squeamishness by individual scribes (B). H is the least plausible of the remaining options because it fabricates an entire esoteric/erotic “nine holes / yin-yang ritual” framework that has no documentary basis in the Bansenshukai, in Fujibayashi’s other writings, or in any contemporary source on kunoichi. It further misattributes the circle glyphs to a mangled Kuji-in diagram—an attribution that collapses under even cursory comparison with actual Kuji-in texts—turning a straightforward textual problem into lurid fantasy. All other choices, while historically debatable, at least remain within the realm of documented ninja practice, Tokugawa politics, or plausible scribal behavior.}

(The answer is H because it alone requires an unsupported sexual-mystical overlay that contradicts the textual and historical record.)

11.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {H}

The pattern of consistent blanks across all surviving copies strongly implies intentional omission/redaction or a deliberate encoding choice at the point of first transcription (or very shortly thereafter). Options A–G, while varying in historical plausibility, at least describe mechanisms that could produce exactly that uniform result: editorial removal, moral self-censorship, later institutional redaction, invisible-ink instructions that later copyists could not reveal, mnemonic placeholders, or physical damage noted by scribes who still recorded the locations.  

H, by contrast, requires an elaborate chain of un-evidenced assumptions: that the circles began as a corrupted Kuji-in diagram, were then re-interpreted as “nine holes”/erotic energy points, and finally degenerated into mere blanks. Nothing in the textual tradition of Bansenshukai or contemporary ninja literature supports an erotic-ritual reading of the kunoichi section, nor does the specific pattern of filled/empty circles match the nine-seal structure of Kuji. The hypothesis is therefore the most speculative and least compatible with the observable evidence.}

9.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {H}

The representation ⬤○○⬤⬤⬤⬤○⬤⬤⬤⬤⬤ is a literal transcription artifact: every historical copy shows the same pattern of solid/empty circles in identical positions, indicating the original kanji were either deliberately omitted or physically absent at the time of copying.  

Evaluating the eight options against known provenance (Fujibayashi’s 1676 compilation for the Toda, the Iga/Ninpiden lineage, the absence of any Oniwaban censorship apparatus before the 18th century, and the documented use of aburidashi or oral mnemonics), H is the only one that requires an entire chain of unsupported assumptions: that the circles are a corrupted Kuji-in diagram, that they encode “nine holes”/erotic Taoist rites, and that this esoteric meaning was then mechanically reduced to blanks. No primary text, no contemporary commentary, and no material evidence supports any of those steps; the claim is therefore the least historically or logically tenable. All other options, while speculative, at least map onto attested practices (redaction, moral squeamishness, physical wear, or simple transcription convention).}

